Accounting Workflow Optimization: Comprehensive Solutions for General and Subsidiary Ledger Management
Roles and Nature of General Ledger and Sub-ledger Accounting
In the financial accounting structure of any enterprise, the relationship between General Ledger (GL) accounting and Subsidiary Ledger (sub-ledger) accounting follows a hierarchical data model from detailed transactions to summarized accounts. Sub-ledger accountants act as the first line of defense, responsible for recording, tracking, and managing specific operational areas such as inventory, accounts receivable (AR), accounts payable (AP), payroll, or fixed assets. Their day-to-day operations are highly transactional and repetitive, demanding high precision and diligence in gathering source documents and journalizing economic events. In contrast, the general accountant acts as a central data hub, receiving and reviewing summarized data from all sub-ledgers to execute month-end and year-end closing adjustments, prepare financial statements, generate management reports, and manage tax compliance. This role demands systemic thinking, a profound grasp of accounting standards, and deep knowledge of tax regulations.   

Despite clear operational division, traditional workflows between these roles often suffer from major bottlenecks. The most common pain point is the data discrepancy between the General Ledger and Subsidiary Ledgers. This mismatch typically stems from processing lags of source documents, manual data entry errors, incorrect double-entry accounts mapping, or a lack of synchronization between disparate software applications. Furthermore, regulatory compliance pressure is mounting, particularly with the enforcement of Tax Administration Law No. 108/2025/QH15 (effective from July 1, 2026), which abolishes flat tax regimes and strictly enforces digitalized, data-driven tax control. Under this modern tax regime, every financial transaction must be captured in real-time with maximum accuracy and transparency. Consequently, workflow optimization is no longer just about internal efficiency; it is an absolute necessity to mitigate legal risks and secure operational stability in the digital era.   

Workflow Optimization for Sub-ledger Accounting Roles
To enhance sub-ledger efficiency, businesses must re-engineer their workflows by standardizing operating procedures (SOPs) and automating repetitive tasks. Instead of passively receiving paper documents, all transactional workflows must be established on the principles of "control at source" and real-time data synchronization.   

For Cash and Bank/Payment Accounting, traditional processes are heavily delayed by physical approval chains and tedious end-of-month bank reconciliations. The optimized workflow relies on a 5-step digitalized payment process. When an expense arises, the requesting employee submits a digital payment request attached with a validated electronic invoice (validated via tax code check). The system automatically cross-references the request with departmental budget limits and routes it online to the Chief Accountant and Board of Directors for approval. Once approved, the payment accountant executes the transfer via electronic banking integrated directly within the ERP, automatically generating journal entries and updating cash/bank registers in real-time. Daily bank reconciliations are automated using fuzzy matching algorithms to immediately isolate mismatches.   

For Sales and Accounts Receivable (AR), the new workflow eliminates revenue recognition lags and mitigates bad debt risks. The process begins with sales order receipt. The system automatically checks inventory availability and the customer’s credit limit before confirming the order. Upon dispatch, an e-invoice is automatically issued and emailed to the customer, simultaneously updating the specific customer's AR account. The AR accountant continuously monitors invoice clearing to match receipts, minimizing end-of-period disputes.   

For Purchase and Accounts Payable (AP), the key is leveraging Artificial Intelligence (AI) to automate invoice processing. When an invoice is received, AI-powered OCR technology extracts critical data points (vendor name, tax code, items, quantities, and total amount). The system then performs a three-way match among the Invoice, Purchase Order (PO), and Goods Receipt Note (GRN). If there is a perfect match, the system automatically posts the journal entries to AP and updates inventory. The AP accountant only intervenes in exceptions where variances exceed pre-defined tolerances, saving over 80% of processing costs.   

For Inventory Accounting, workflows are upgraded through barcode/RFID integration. Upon physical warehouse entry, the warehouse keeper scans the item tag, automatically creating a GRN. The system immediately journalizes the inventory increase and calculates unit costs using weighted average or FIFO methods. Monthly physical counts are supported by handheld scanners connected directly to the database, allowing rapid comparison with ledger balances, auto-generating variance reports, and proposing adjustments for shrinkages.   

For Payroll Accounting, workflows are optimized by importing timecard data from automated attendance systems. Daily attendance logs are consolidated and routed to the payroll engine. The engine calculates base salaries, overtime premiums, progressive personal income tax (PIT) withholdings, and statutory social insurance contributions based on predefined compliance rules. Once approved online, the system exports bank transfer files and automatically posts payroll expenses to matching cost centers, cutting payroll processing time from days to hours.   

For Fixed Assets and Prepaid Expenses, workflows are automated to handle the entire asset lifecycle from acquisition, revaluation, monthly depreciation, to disposal. When an asset is registered, it is assigned a unique tracking code and a pre-configured depreciation schedule. Every month, the system runs depreciation and prepaid amortization routines without manual intervention, ensuring consistency and compliance with state regulations.   

Standardization of General Accounting and Closing Workflows
The optimized sub-ledger environment serves as a stable foundation for the general accountant. Instead of spending hours correcting sub-ledger errors, the general accountant focuses on control, financial analysis, and corporate governance. Their workflow centers on two core pillars: continuous data control and automated closing routines.   

Continuous data control utilizes automated ledger audits. Daily or weekly, the general accountant runs automated exception reports to identify unposted vouchers, negative cash balances, or mismatches between VAT declarations and corresponding GL balances. To ensure integrity, the GL audit must satisfy the fundamental balancing principles of double-entry accounting :   

Total Opening Debit Balance (General Ledger)=Total Opening Credit Balance (General Ledger)
Total Period Debit Occurrence (General Ledger)=Total Period Credit Occurrence (General Ledger)=Total Journal Occurrence
Total Closing Debit Balance (General Ledger)=Total Closing Credit Balance (General Ledger)
The month-end, quarter-end, and year-end closing process—traditionally a major source of stress—is transformed into a highly structured, automated 6-step workflow overseen by the general accountant. First, the system calculates inventory cost of goods sold (COGS) under the selected costing method (WAC or FIFO) and posts the corresponding entries. Second, logistics and freight costs are allocated into imported inventory values. Third, depreciation and prepaid amortization routines are executed. Fourth, input and output VAT balances are cleared to determine net VAT payable or deductible. Fifth, progressive PIT amounts are finalized and posted.   

Sixth, the system executes closing journal entries to transfer balances from revenue, other income, and expense accounts to the profit-and-loss summary account :   

Revenue & Other Income (Accounts Type 5, 7)⟶P&L Summary Account (Account 911)
COGS, Finance, Selling, & Admin Expenses (Accounts Type 6, 8)⟶P&L Summary Account (Account 911)
The system then automatically computes corporate income tax (CIT) according to current tax regulations and posts the tax expense :   

CIT Expense Account (Account 821)⟶CIT Payable Account (Account 3334)
Finally, the remaining balance on the P&L summary account (net profit or loss) is transferred to retained earnings :   

P&L Summary Account (Account 911)⟶Retained Earnings Account (Account 421)
Upon completing these steps, the system clears temporary data caches, freezes the closing period to block further modifications, and sends an automated success alert to the Chief Accountant.   

Event-Driven Architecture and Real-Time Sync
Seamless, zero-latency coordination between sub-ledger and general accountants is guaranteed by Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) and Realtime Sync.   

At the UI layer, when a sub-ledger accountant creates a transaction (e.g., GRN, cash payment), frontend validation checks mathematical balance and data completeness. The client then transmits a secure API request. On the backend, a database transaction is initiated with serializable isolation. Within this transaction, the server validates formatting, checks the lock date, and confirms opening balances. Upon validation, data is written to the database master and detail tables, an audit log is created, and the transaction is committed.   

Immediately after DB commit, the server publishes a real-time event via Redis and WebSockets. To ensure strict data isolation in a multi-tenant environment, users are automatically grouped into WebSocket rooms based on their companyId. Real-time events are broadcasted exclusively within the tenant's room, preventing cross-tenant leaks.   

On the receiving side, the general accountant's client continuously listens for events. To prevent client-side overhead from high concurrent transactions, the system filters out self-generated events and applies rate-limiting (max 30 events per 5 seconds, minimum 80ms interval). Validated events trigger a query invalidation hook with a 250ms debounce time, forcing the general accountant's dashboards (cash flow, ledger, inventory balance) to dynamically refresh from the server, establishing real-time synchronization.   

